WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
 If you haven't heard your adult child's voice in months, or years,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
 I want you to hear one sentence before anything else.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.000
 Your child is not coming back on their own,

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.000
 and that is not the end of the story.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.000
 I know both halves of that sentence are hard to hear.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.000
 The first half, because you've been waiting,

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.000
 and the second half, because hope has burned you before.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.000
 Stay with me for the next few minutes anyway.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.000
 I'm going to tell you what the silence is actually made of,

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.000
 and what 40 years of sitting with estranged families

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.000
 taught me about the way back.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.000
 My name is Catherine Taylor Hickem.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.000
 I'm a licensed clinical social worker,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.000
 and I've spent over 40 years as a psychotherapist,

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.000
 and more than 20 as a family and executive coach,

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.000
 sitting with families in their hardest moments.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
 I wrote a book years ago called Regret Free Parenting

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000
 about raising young children with intention,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.000
 but the reason I understand estrangement isn't my license.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.000
 It's that I was the child on the other side of the silence.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000
 For the first five years of my life, I had an amazing dad.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.000
 He was a minister, tender, sweet, gentle, bedtime stories every night,

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.000
 and then in a single moment, everything changed.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.000
 One Sunday evening, five-year-old me quietly walked a visiting little girl

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
 to the bathroom in the middle of my father's sermon.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.000
 He stopped.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.000
 "In front of the whole congregation," he said,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.000
 "Cathy Taylor, you sit down, and don't you ever do that again."

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
 My heart had been to help somebody, but in that moment,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.000
 my relationship with my father changed.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.000
 The bedtime story stopped.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.000
 He became distant and hard, and for the next ten years,

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.000
 my goal in my own home was to be invisible.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.000
 At 15, I did something I still can't believe I did.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.000
 I made an appointment with his church secretary for four o'clock

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:06.000
 on a Monday afternoon to talk to my dad about our relationship.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000
 He looked at me and said, "Get out of here, go home."

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.000
 We didn't have that conversation for another ten years.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.000
 At 25, after college, marriage, graduate school,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.000
 and years of my own personal therapy, I tried one more time.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.000
 I told him, "I want a relationship with you,

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.000
 but it can't be the way we've always had it."

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.000
 And my father looked at me and said, "Cathy, I made a lot of mistakes with you.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.000
 I can't undo those.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
 The only thing I can do is give you my word

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.000
 that I will be the dad that you need and not the dad that you had."

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.000
 And for the next 33 years, I had the most amazing father.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.000
 He was interested in who I was.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.000
 He was respectful of my boundaries.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.000
 He was always very fully present with me,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.000
 and my children adored him.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.000
 Those years taught me the two truths everything I do is built on.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.000
 One, it is never too late to grow and change,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.000
 and it only takes one person to change your relationship.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:12.000
 Since you're the one watching this, you get to be that one.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.000
 Now let me tell you what 40 years of clinical work showed me

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.000
 about estrangement because it explains why everything you've tried has failed

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.000
 and none of it was your fault.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.000
 Estrangement is not a communication problem.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.000
 It's a safety problem.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.000
 Your child isn't staying away because they need more time

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.000
 or because the two of you haven't found the right words.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.000
 They're staying away because nothing has changed

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.000
 that makes contact feel emotionally safe.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.000
 That's why the letters go unanswered.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:48.000
 That's why giving them space only hardens the silence.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.000
 Time by itself is not medicine here,

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.000
 and that's why therapy so often fails for estrangement.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.000
 It treats a safety problem as a communication problem,

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.000
 and then everyone concludes the family is hopeless.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.000
 The family was never hopeless.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.000
 The diagnosis was wrong.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.000
 Here's the frame that makes it all make sense.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.000
 Every relationship runs on an account,

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.000
 not a ledger of favors, an account of safety.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.000
 Every interaction is either a deposit, contact with you,

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.000
 cost your children nothing, or gave them something, or a withdrawal.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.000
 Contact requires armor, management, defense.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.000
 Estrangement is what happens when that account doesn't just run low.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.000
 It goes overdrawn, and the account holder closes the branch.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:42.000
 Now, look at what most of us do next, the card, the forwarded article,

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.000
 the long message written at midnight.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.000
 I call it the overreach reflex, and it isn't a strategy.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.000
 It's what a parent's nervous system does when a child is missing,

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.000
 but every reach out is a request for contact,

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.000
 and a request for contact draws on the account.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.000
 A reach out before the safety work is a withdrawal from an overdrawn account.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.000
 This is why the perfect letter you keep trying to write does not exist.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.000
 It's not that your words are wrong.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:16.000
 It's that words are withdrawals until the account can bear them.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.000
 So if reaching out isn't step one, what is?

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.000
 Diagnosis.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.000
 In 40 years, I've watched every estrangement sort into one of six patterns.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.000
 The slow fade, where there was no fight.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:32.000
 The calls that just got shorter.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.000
 The rupture.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.000
 Everyone can name the date.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:39.000
 The rewrite, where a letter arrives describing a childhood you don't recognize.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.000
 The new household, where the distance came with a person,

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:46.000
 often a son or a daughter-in-law at the door.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.000
 And there's always the shielded door and the standoff.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.000
 Why does the pattern matter?

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.000
 Because the right next step is different in each one.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:00.000
 And in some steps that heals one pattern may actively damage another.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.000
 A heartfelt apology letter is medicine in one pattern.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.000
 And gasoline in two others.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.000
 And the silence itself comes in three kinds.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:14.000
 Testing, protective, and hardened, each with its own honest timeline.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.000
 Parents who skip the diagnosis do the right things in the wrong pattern

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.000
 and then conclude that nothing works.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.000
 Nothing was ever going to work.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:25.000
 It was the wrong pattern.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.000
 That's why I wrote Welcome Home.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.000
 It's not a book of comfort.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.000
 There are plenty of those.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.000
 It's a path.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:38.000
 An ordered, step-by-step path that begins with you rather than your child.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.000
 Which means you can start today, even in total silence.

